1
00:00:03,439 --> 00:00:05,496
Ruth: Alexa, my grandson just called.

2
00:00:05,496 --> 00:00:09,943
Ruth: He's in jail and needs two thousand dollars in gift cards for bail.

3
00:00:10,743 --> 00:00:12,387
Alexa: I'm glad you asked me first.

4
00:00:12,387 --> 00:00:16,262
Alexa: The emergency story and the gift cards are common signs of a scam.

5
00:00:16,262 --> 00:00:20,079
Alexa: Should I text Michael to check, and tell Sarah you got this call?

6
00:00:20,879 --> 00:00:21,599
Ruth: Yes.

7
00:00:22,399 --> 00:00:25,231
Alexa: Done. I'll tell you when Michael answers.

8
00:00:27,831 --> 00:00:31,997
Alexa: Michael says he did not call you, so you did the right thing by checking.

9
00:00:31,997 --> 00:00:35,364
Alexa: Please don't send any money, and hang up if they call back.

10
00:00:35,364 --> 00:00:37,647
Alexa: Would you like help reporting this call?

11
00:00:37,997 --> 00:00:41,981
Scam Guardian is an Alexa+ add-on for the moment right after a scary call.

12
00:00:42,197 --> 00:00:47,066
The FTC's advice is simple: hang up, and check with your family on a number you know.

13
00:00:47,066 --> 00:00:50,045
Scam Guardian turns that advice into a conversation.

14
00:00:50,447 --> 00:00:51,839
Ruth: So can I pay him?

15
00:00:52,639 --> 00:00:57,631
Alexa: Let's not send any money yet. First, call your family on a number you know.

16
00:00:58,431 --> 00:01:01,455
Ruth: He's still on the other phone, he says I have to hurry.

17
00:01:02,255 --> 00:01:07,919
Alexa: You can hang up now. A real family member will understand. Then tell me what happened.

18
00:01:08,719 --> 00:01:11,599
Ruth: My card number is four one two two three three

19
00:01:12,399 --> 00:01:13,705
Alexa: Let me stop you there.

20
00:01:13,705 --> 00:01:18,927
Alexa: Please don't share numbers like that with me or anyone on the phone. We don't need them.

21
00:01:19,727 --> 00:01:22,847
Ruth: There's a man at my door, he says he's here for the money.

22
00:01:23,647 --> 00:01:29,143
Alexa: Don't open the door and don't hand over money. If you feel unsafe, call 911 now.

23
00:01:29,793 --> 00:01:33,495
It never says a payment is safe. It never contacts the caller.

24
00:01:33,495 --> 00:01:37,017
It never takes card numbers. And it never records the call.

25
00:01:37,042 --> 00:01:38,866
Ruth: Yes, help me report it.

26
00:01:39,666 --> 00:01:44,610
Alexa: I put a summary on the screen with where to report it. Nothing was sent to any agency.

27
00:01:46,984 --> 00:01:49,576
Ruth: I already bought the cards and read him the numbers.

28
00:01:50,376 --> 00:01:54,866
Alexa: Thank you for telling me. Call the company that sold the gift card right away.

29
00:01:54,866 --> 00:01:58,032
Alexa: Should I tell Sarah you got this call, so she can help?

30
00:01:58,832 --> 00:01:59,552
Ruth: Yes.

31
00:02:00,352 --> 00:02:02,209
Alexa: Done, I let Sarah know.

32
00:02:02,209 --> 00:02:08,426
Alexa: The National Elder Fraud Hotline at 833 372 8311 gives free help on weekdays.

33
00:02:08,426 --> 00:02:11,656
Alexa: Would you like help reporting this call?

34
00:02:13,306 --> 00:02:16,450
No blame, one first step, and the family knows.

35
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:21,622
A grandchild sets it up from their phone: who Alexa can check with, and who gets a heads up.

36
00:02:21,622 --> 00:02:24,694
An optional family password, and a history of every check.

37
00:02:27,764 --> 00:02:31,858
Under the hood is a real MCP server with eight tools, on AWS Lambda.

38
00:02:31,858 --> 00:02:34,207
The safety rules live inside the tools.

39
00:02:34,207 --> 00:02:41,372
Rules answer what must be exact, and Claude Haiku on Amazon Bedrock answers the open questions, through the same guard.

40
00:02:41,764 --> 00:02:47,687
Seventy five adversarial phrases run on every push, and the scam patterns are open source.

41
00:02:47,687 --> 00:02:50,188
Hang up. Ask Alexa. Check with family.
